- **Shape.** On the 4 inputs every method leaves valid, the optimizer (alone or combined) has the lowest mean phi, the quantity it minimises. Winslow comes within 0.001 of it on the annulus and sphere, in 45 % of its time. By min scaled J, Laplace wins on the annulus, free disk and graded layer, the optimizer on the sphere: the scaled J reads angles only.
- **Concave outline.** The L-shape's Coons patch starts with 10 inverted quads. Laplace leaves 6, the optimizer alone 9, the combined schedule 3; Winslow leaves 1, only at the reflex corner, where two quads share a 270-degree turn.
- **Size.** No method evens element size everywhere: Laplace has the lowest spread of area (CV) on the annulus and L-shape, another method on the free disk, tangled square, sphere and graded layer.
- **Tangled squares.** Of 8 tangled squares, Laplace repaired 8, Winslow 8, the optimizer alone 0 (at the default `delta` it stops after 1 iteration), the combined schedule 8.
- **Free boundary.** The disk has `boundary="tangent"`: a boundary vertex slides along the boundary and never moves inward, so the area is kept (Laplace +0.0 %, Winslow +0.0 %, optimizer +0.0 %). The optimizer stalls there, though: it stops after 14 iterations at min scaled J 0.617, about a frozen boundary's 0.610, while `boundary="slide"` reaches 0.856. With `boundary="free"` Laplace pulls the boundary onto its chords (-15.7 %) and the optimizer lets the rim cells grow (+61.7 %). When the optimizer must move a boundary, use `"slide"` or `OnCurve` ({doc}`constraints`).
- **Grading.** Every method thickens the graded layer: Laplace +229 %, Winslow +207 % (its guard stopped it after 3 sweeps), the optimizer +584 %; see {doc}`grading`.
- **Cost.** A Laplace pass costs 0.4 ms, a Winslow sweep 0.9 ms, an optimizer iteration 4.3 ms with its line search. Medians over the inputs; single runs on a laptop, running serial.
